    solution_walkthrough: "**Identify the problem type** Relative velocity in two dimensions (frame addition).\n\n**Governing principle** Galilean velocity addition: velocity relative to ground = velocity relative to water + velocity of water relative to ground. Perpendicular components are independent.\n\n**Assumptions** Uniform current, constant swimming speed, swimmer holds a due-north heading.\n\n**Execute** Her northward (cross-stream) velocity is the full $4.0\\ \\text{m/s}$ because the current is purely eastward and does not help or hinder the crossing. Crossing time: $$t = \\frac{80}{4.0} = 20\\ \\text{s}.$$ Downstream drift: $d = 3.0 \\times 20 = 60\\ \\text{m}$. Ground speed combines the perpendicular vectors: $\\sqrt{4.0^2 + 3.0^2} = 5.0\\ \\text{m/s}$.\n\n**Answer** $t = 20\\ \\text{s}$, drift $= 60\\ \\text{m}$, ground speed $= 5.0\\ \\text{m/s}$.\n\n**Sanity check** The classic 3-4-5 triangle appears; crossing time depends only on the cross-stream component.\n\n**How an interviewer probes this** They may ask what heading she needs to land directly across with no downstream drift, and how long that would take.",
