    solution_walkthrough: "**Identify the problem type** One-dimensional free fall from rest, using average velocity over an interval.\n\n**Governing principle** Newton's Second Law with constant $a = g$; average velocity over an interval equals instantaneous velocity at the interval's midpoint in time.\n\n**Assumptions** No air resistance; stone released from rest; $g = 10\\ \\text{m/s}^2$.\n\n**Execute** Average speed passing the window: $\\bar v = 2.0/0.20 = 10\\ \\text{m/s}$, which is the speed at the middle of the $0.20\\ \\text{s}$ interval. The speed at the top of the window is $0.10\\ \\text{s}$ earlier: $v_{top} = 10 - g(0.10) = 9.0\\ \\text{m/s}$. Height fallen to reach that speed from rest: $$h = \\frac{v_{top}^2}{2g} = \\frac{9.0^2}{2 \\times 10} \\approx 4.05\\ \\text{m}.$$\n\n**Answer** About $4.1\\ \\text{m}$ above the top of the window.\n\n**Sanity check** $9\\ \\text{m/s}$ after roughly $0.9\\ \\text{s}$ of fall matches $v = gt$, consistent.\n\n**How an interviewer probes this** They may ask for the total time of fall from release to the window top and confirm it with $v = gt$.",
