    time_limit_seconds: null,
    parts_json: null,
    tier: "intermediate",
    solution_walkthrough: "**Identify the problem type** Ballistic pendulum - inelastic collision followed by an energy-conserving swing.\n\n**Governing principle** Conservation of Momentum for the impact; Conservation of Energy for the swing. You cannot use energy through the collision (KE is lost).\n\n**Assumptions** Bullet embeds instantly, cord massless, no air drag, swing height measured to center of mass.\n\n**Execute** Swing phase (energy): the combined mass's KE converts to PE.\n$$V = \\sqrt{2gh} = \\sqrt{2(9.8)(0.08)} \\approx 1.25\\,\\text{m/s}$$\nImpact phase (momentum), with $m=0.020\\,\\text{kg}$, $M=2.0\\,\\text{kg}$:\n$$v = \\frac{(M+m)V}{m} = \\frac{(2.02)(1.25)}{0.020} \\approx 126\\,\\text{m/s}$$\n\n**Answer** $v \\approx 126\\,\\text{m/s}$.\n\n**Sanity check** Bullet is $\\sim100\\times$ lighter, so its speed should be $\\sim100\\times$ the block's - matches.\n\n**How an interviewer probes this** 'Why can't you set the bullet's initial KE equal to the final PE?' Because the embedding collision dissipates energy.",
    assumption_challenges: ["You assumed no air resistance during the swing - with drag, would the measured height over- or under-estimate the true bullet speed?"],
