    solution_walkthrough: "**Identify the problem type** Head-on elastic collision, one target initially at rest.\n\n**Governing principle** Conservation of Momentum AND Conservation of Kinetic Energy (both hold for elastic collisions).\n\n**Assumptions** Perfectly elastic, motion along a line, no friction.\n\n**Execute** Use the standard elastic-collision results (derived from the two conservation laws):\n$$v_1' = \\frac{m_1-m_2}{m_1+m_2}\\,v_1 = \\frac{2-1}{3}(6) = 2\\,\\text{m/s}$$\n$$v_2' = \\frac{2m_1}{m_1+m_2}\\,v_1 = \\frac{4}{3}(6) = 8\\,\\text{m/s}$$\n\n**Answer** Incoming ball: $2\\,\\text{m/s}$ forward; target ball: $8\\,\\text{m/s}$ forward.\n\n**Sanity check** Momentum: $2(2)+1(8)=12=2(6)$. KE: $\\tfrac12 2(4)+\\tfrac12 1(64)=36=\\tfrac12 2(36)$. Both conserved.\n\n**How an interviewer probes this** 'What if the masses were equal?' Then the velocities fully exchange - the classic Newton's-cradle result.",
