    solution_walkthrough: "**Identify the problem type** An explosion (reverse collision) from rest; find one unknown fragment velocity.\n\n**Governing principle** Conservation of Momentum: total momentum is zero before, so it must sum to zero after.\n\n**Assumptions** Explosion is instantaneous, external forces (gravity) negligible during the blast, fragments are point masses.\n\n**Execute** Third mass $m_3 = 10 - 3 - 4 = 3\\,\\text{kg}$. Known momenta: north $= 3(20) = 60$, east $= 4(15) = 60\\,\\text{kg·m/s}$. For the total to vanish:\n$$p_{3x} = -60,\\quad p_{3y} = -60\\,\\text{kg·m/s}$$\n$$|p_3| = \\sqrt{60^2 + 60^2} = 84.85\\,\\text{kg·m/s}$$\n$$v_3 = 84.85 / 3 \\approx 28.3\\,\\text{m/s}$$\nDirection: $\\arctan(60/60) = 45^\\circ$ into the third quadrant, i.e. southwest.\n\n**Answer** $v_3 \\approx 28.3\\,\\text{m/s}$, directed $45^\\circ$ south of west.\n\n**Sanity check** The vector sum of all three momenta returns to zero, and the lightest fragment carrying the recoil moves fastest, as expected.\n\n**How an interviewer probes this** Expect: where did the kinetic energy come from, since initial KE was zero? (Stored chemical energy.)",
