    solution_walkthrough: "**Identify the problem type** Static equilibrium check on an incline with friction.\n\n**Governing principle** Newton's Second Law: the block stays at rest only if static friction can balance the gravity component along the slope.\n\n**Assumptions** Rigid block, point-mass, $\\mu_s$ uniform, ramp does not deform.\n\n**Execute** Resolve gravity into components. Along the incline: $mg\\sin\\theta = 4(9.8)(0.5) = 19.6\\,\\text{N}$. Normal force: $N = mg\\cos\\theta = 4(9.8)(0.866) = 33.9\\,\\text{N}$. Maximum static friction: $f_{max} = \\mu_s N = 0.5(33.9) = 17.0\\,\\text{N}$.\n\n**Answer** Since $19.6\\,\\text{N} > 17.0\\,\\text{N}$, friction cannot hold the block; it slides down.\n\n**Sanity check** The block slides whenever $\\tan\\theta > \\mu_s$; here $\\tan 30^\\circ = 0.577 > 0.50$, confirming motion.\n\n**How an interviewer probes this** They may ask: once moving, what is the acceleration if kinetic friction is $\\mu_k = 0.40$?",
