    time_limit_seconds: null,
    parts_json: null,
    tier: "intermediate",
    solution_walkthrough: "**Identify the problem type** Connected masses over an ideal pulley (Atwood machine).\n\n**Governing principle** Newton's Second Law applied separately to each mass, with a shared magnitude of acceleration and equal tension throughout a massless cord.\n\n**Assumptions** Massless, frictionless pulley; inextensible massless cord; equal tension on both sides.\n\n**Execute** For the heavier mass (descending): $m_1 g - T = m_1 a$. For the lighter (rising): $T - m_2 g = m_2 a$. Add: $(m_1 - m_2)g = (m_1 + m_2)a$, so $a = \\frac{(6-4)(9.8)}{10} = 1.96\\,\\text{m/s}^2$. Then $T = m_2(g + a) = 4(9.8 + 1.96) = 47.0\\,\\text{N}$.\n\n**Answer** $a = 1.96\\,\\text{m/s}^2$, $T \\approx 47.0\\,\\text{N}$.\n\n**Sanity check** $T$ lies between $m_2 g = 39.2\\,\\text{N}$ and $m_1 g = 58.8\\,\\text{N}$, as it must for a moving system.\n\n**How an interviewer probes this** They may ask why the tension is not simply the average of the two weights, or what happens as $m_1 \\to m_2$.",
