    solution_walkthrough: "**Identify the problem type** Equilibrium (constant velocity) on an incline with kinetic friction.\n\n**Governing principle** Newton's Second Law with $a = 0$: tension must balance both the gravity component along the slope and kinetic friction, which opposes the upward motion.\n\n**Assumptions** Rope parallel to incline; $\\mu_k$ constant; crate is a rigid point-mass; no air resistance.\n\n**Execute** Normal force: $N = mg\\cos\\theta = 10(9.8)(0.906) = 88.8\\,\\text{N}$. Gravity along slope: $mg\\sin\\theta = 10(9.8)(0.423) = 41.4\\,\\text{N}$. Friction (down the slope, opposing motion): $f = \\mu_k N = 0.30(88.8) = 26.6\\,\\text{N}$. Constant velocity means $T = mg\\sin\\theta + f = 41.4 + 26.6 = 68.0\\,\\text{N}$.\n\n**Answer** $T \\approx 68.0\\,\\text{N}$.\n\n**Sanity check** Without friction, $T$ would be $41.4\\,\\text{N}$; friction adds resistance, so a larger tension is expected.\n\n**How an interviewer probes this** They may ask how the required tension changes if the rope is instead angled above the incline surface.",
