    solution_walkthrough: "**Identify the problem type** Single block on an incline; must first test the static threshold, then compute kinetic motion.\n\n**Governing principle** Newton's Second Law resolved along and perpendicular to the incline.\n\n**Assumptions** Point mass, no other applied force, $g = 10\\,\\text{m/s}^2$, $\\cos 30^\\circ \\approx 0.866$.\n\n**Execute** Driving force down-slope: $$mg\\sin 30^\\circ = 5\\times10\\times0.5 = 25\\,\\text{N}.$$ Maximum static friction: $$\\mu_s mg\\cos 30^\\circ = 0.5\\times5\\times10\\times0.866 \\approx 21.7\\,\\text{N}.$$ Since $25 > 21.7$, the block slides. Using kinetic friction, $$a = g(\\sin 30^\\circ - \\mu_k\\cos 30^\\circ) = 10(0.5 - 0.3\\times0.866) \\approx 2.4\\,\\text{m/s}^2.$$\n\n**Answer** The block slides down at $a \\approx 2.4\\,\\text{m/s}^2$.\n\n**Sanity check** Kinetic friction $\\approx 13\\,\\text{N}$ opposes $25\\,\\text{N}$; net $12\\,\\text{N}$ over $5\\,\\text{kg}$ gives $2.4\\,\\text{m/s}^2$. Consistent.\n\n**How an interviewer probes this** They ask why you compared against $\\mu_s$ not $\\mu_k$ first, testing that you know static must be exceeded before motion begins.",
