    solution_walkthrough: "**Identify the problem type** A spinning-body problem with no external torque: an angular momentum conservation question.\n\n**Governing principle** Conservation of Angular Momentum: $I_1\\omega_1 = I_2\\omega_2$.\n\n**Assumptions** Frictionless ice (no external torque about the spin axis), rigid redistribution of mass.\n\n**Execute** Solve for the new speed: $$\\omega_2 = \\frac{I_1}{I_2}\\,\\omega_1 = \\frac{6}{2}(2) = 6\\ \\text{rev/s}.$$ For energy, use $KE = \\tfrac12 I\\omega^2 = \\tfrac12 L\\omega$. Since $L$ is fixed and $\\omega$ triples, $$\\frac{KE_2}{KE_1} = \\frac{\\omega_2}{\\omega_1} = 3.$$\n\n**Answer** $\\omega_2 = 6\\ \\text{rev/s}$; kinetic energy triples.\n\n**Sanity check** Pulling mass inward must speed the spin (like water down a drain); energy isn't conserved because her muscles do work against centripetal demands.\n\n**How an interviewer probes this** Expect: \"You said $L$ is conserved but $KE$ is not — reconcile that.\" Tie the energy jump to internal muscular work, not a violation of any law.",
