    solution_walkthrough: "**Identify the problem type** A rolling-without-slipping race down an incline — an energy-partition problem.\n\n**Governing principle** Conservation of Energy, splitting gravitational PE into translational and rotational KE.\n\n**Assumptions** Rolling without slipping ($v=\\omega R$), no slipping losses, uniform mass distribution.\n\n**Execute** With $I = c\\,mR^2$: $$mgh = \\tfrac12 mv^2 + \\tfrac12 I\\omega^2 = \\tfrac12 mv^2(1+c).$$ So $v = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{2gh}{1+c}}$. Solid cylinder $c=\\tfrac12 \\Rightarrow v=\\sqrt{4gh/3}$; hoop $c=1 \\Rightarrow v=\\sqrt{gh}$.\n\n**Answer** The solid cylinder arrives first. $$\\frac{v_{\\text{solid}}}{v_{\\text{hoop}}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{1+1}{1+1/2}}=\\sqrt{\\tfrac43}\\approx 1.15.$$\n\n**Sanity check** Mass and radius cancel — only the shape factor $c$ matters. The hoop stores more energy in rotation, leaving less for translation, so it's slower. Correct.\n\n**How an interviewer probes this** \"Does the answer change if you double the mass, or the radius?\" It doesn't — a key insight they want you to state explicitly.",
