    solution_walkthrough: "**Identify the problem type** A rigid body released from rest about a fixed pivot — a rotational Newton's second law (torque) problem.\n\n**Governing principle** Newton's Second Law for rotation: $\\tau = I\\alpha$.\n\n**Assumptions** Uniform rod (weight acts at center $L/2$), frictionless pivot, evaluate at the instant of release when the rod is horizontal.\n\n**Execute** Torque about the pivot: $$\\tau = mg\\cdot\\tfrac{L}{2}.$$ Then $$\\alpha = \\frac{\\tau}{I} = \\frac{mgL/2}{\\tfrac13 mL^2} = \\frac{3g}{2L} = \\frac{3(9.8)}{2(2)} \\approx 7.4\\ \\text{rad/s}^2.$$ The free end's tangential acceleration is $a = \\alpha L = \\tfrac{3g}{2} \\approx 14.7\\ \\text{m/s}^2$.\n\n**Answer** $\\alpha \\approx 7.4\\ \\text{rad/s}^2$; free-end acceleration $\\approx 14.7\\ \\text{m/s}^2$.\n\n**Sanity check** The tip accelerates at $1.5g$ — faster than free fall. That famous result (a coin on the tip gets left behind) confirms the setup.\n\n**How an interviewer probes this** \"Why does the tip beat gravity but the center doesn't?\" Compute the center's acceleration ($\\tfrac34 g$) to show the crossover point at $2L/3$.",
