    solution_walkthrough: "**Identify the problem type** A person jumping onto a rotating platform — an inelastic angular collision, so use angular momentum conservation.\n\n**Governing principle** Conservation of Angular Momentum about the vertical axis (the landing forces act through/around the axle, delivering no external torque about it).\n\n**Assumptions** Impulsive landing (position fixed during collision), child modeled as a point mass at radius $R$, frictionless axle.\n\n**Execute** Initial angular momentum is the child's about the axis: $L_i = mvR$. After landing the combined inertia is $I + mR^2$: $$mvR = (I + mR^2)\\,\\omega.$$ $$\\omega = \\frac{(40)(3)(2)}{200 + (40)(2^2)} = \\frac{240}{360} \\approx 0.67\\ \\text{rad/s}.$$\n\n**Answer** $\\omega \\approx 0.67\\ \\text{rad/s}$.\n\n**Sanity check** Units are $\\text{rad/s}$, and the child's added inertia $mR^2 = 160$ is comparable to $I=200$, so a modest spin-up is expected. Kinetic energy drops (inelastic), as it should.\n\n**How an interviewer probes this** \"Was linear momentum conserved here?\" No — the axle exerts an external force; only angular momentum about the axis is conserved.",
