    solution_walkthrough: "**Identify the problem type** A floating-vessel loading problem: added weight is supported by added displacement.\n\n**Governing principle** Archimedes' Principle: each increment of cargo weight is balanced by the weight of the extra water displaced as the draft increases.\n\n**Assumptions** Vertical walls (constant cross-section over the draft range), still water, barge stays upright and doesn't overtop.\n\n**Execute** The waterplane area is $A=6\\times3=18\\ \\mathrm{m^2}$. Increase in draft $\\Delta d=0.90-0.30=0.60\\ \\mathrm{m}$. Extra displaced volume $\\Delta V=A\\,\\Delta d=18\\times0.60=10.8\\ \\mathrm{m^3}$. Added mass $\\Delta m=\\rho\\,\\Delta V=1000\\times10.8$.\n\n**Answer** About $10{,}800\\ \\mathrm{kg}$ ($10.8\\ \\mathrm{t}$) of cargo.\n\n**Sanity check** Each $1\\ \\mathrm{m^2}$ of waterplane sunk $1\\ \\mathrm{m}$ carries $1000\\ \\mathrm{kg}$; $18\\times0.6=10.8\\ \\mathrm{m^3}$ of water gives $10.8\\ \\mathrm{t}$ - reasonable.\n\n**How an interviewer probes this** Follow-up: if this barge were floated in seawater instead, would it carry the same cargo at that draft?",
    assumption_challenges: ["You assumed vertical walls giving a constant waterplane area - if the hull flares outward with draft, does the barge carry more or less cargo for the same draft change?"],
