    solution_walkthrough: "**Identify the problem type** A two-body gravitational free-fall where both masses move, so I must track the kinetic energy of both bodies plus momentum symmetry.\n\n**Governing principle** Conservation of Energy, with Conservation of Momentum enforcing equal speeds.\n\n**Assumptions** Isolated system (no external forces), point masses, starting from rest so total momentum stays zero.\n\n**Execute** Zero total momentum and equal masses mean each asteroid always has the same speed $v$ in opposite directions; sketch the two masses approaching a fixed midpoint. Total kinetic energy is $2\\cdot\\tfrac{1}{2}mv^2 = mv^2$. Using $U = -\\frac{Gm^2}{s}$, the potential energy released going from separation $d$ to $d/2$ is $U(d) - U(d/2) = -\\frac{Gm^2}{d} + \\frac{Gm^2}{d/2} = \\frac{Gm^2}{d}$. Setting released PE equal to total KE gives $mv^2 = \\frac{Gm^2}{d}$, so $v^2 = \\frac{Gm}{d}$.\n\n**Answer** $v = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{Gm}{d}}$ for each asteroid.\n\n**Sanity check** The energy is shared by both bodies (total KE is $mv^2$, not $\\tfrac{1}{2}mv^2$); assigning all released PE to one body would wrongly inflate $v$.\n\n**How an interviewer probes this** Expect: \"How would the answer change if one asteroid were much more massive?\"",
