    solution_walkthrough: "**Identify the problem type** Volume-ratio Fermi estimate. **Governing principle** Dimensional Analysis: count $=V_{\\text{container}}/V_{\\text{effective per ball}}$. **Assumptions** Bus interior $\\approx 12\\ \\text{m}\\times2.5\\ \\text{m}\\times2\\ \\text{m}$; approximate each ball as occupying a cube of side $d$ (this bakes in $\\sim35\\%$ empty space, close to real random packing). **Execute** $V_{\\text{bus}}\\approx60\\ \\text{m}^3$. Cube per ball: $(0.04\\ \\text{m})^3=6.4\\times10^{-5}\\ \\text{m}^3$. So $N\\approx\\frac{60}{6.4\\times10^{-5}}\\approx 9\\times10^5$. **Answer** $N\\sim10^6$ ping-pong balls. **Sanity check** A million is huge but a bus is $\\sim10^6$ times a small ball's volume, so the magnitude is reasonable. **How an interviewer probes this** They may ask how using true sphere volume ($\\frac{\\pi}{6}d^3$) plus a packing fraction of $0.64$ changes the count.",
