    solution_walkthrough: "**Identify the problem type** Fixed mass of gas in a rigid container heated: a Gay-Lussac (constant-volume) process.\n\n**Governing principle** Ideal Gas Law $PV=nRT$; with $V$, $n$ constant, $P/T=\\text{const}$.\n\n**Assumptions** Ideal gas, no leakage, rigid walls so $V$ fixed.\n\n**Execute** Convert to absolute pressure first: $P_1=200+100=300\\,\\text{kPa}$. Then\n$$P_2=P_1\\frac{T_2}{T_1}=300\\cdot\\frac{600}{300}=600\\,\\text{kPa abs}.$$\nGauge: $600-100=500\\,\\text{kPa}$.\n\n**Answer** $600\\,\\text{kPa}$ absolute, $500\\,\\text{kPa}$ gauge.\n\n**Sanity check** Doubling absolute temperature doubles absolute pressure, exactly as expected.\n\n**How an interviewer probes this** Expect: \"Why can't you just double the gauge pressure?\" The gas law only works with absolute pressure and absolute (Kelvin) temperature.",
