    solution_walkthrough: "**Identify the problem type** A related-rates problem with two competing volume flows.\n\n**Governing principle** Related rates via implicit differentiation of the conical volume, using net flow.\n\n**Assumptions** $r=h$ throughout; leak law $Q_{out}=2h$ holds; water incompressible.\n\n**Execute** Net rate: $\\frac{dV}{dt}=8-2h$. Volume of cone with $r=h$: $V=\\frac{\\pi}{3}h^3$, so $\\frac{dV}{dt}=\\pi h^2\\frac{dh}{dt}$. Set equal:\n$$8-2h=\\pi h^2\\frac{dh}{dt}.$$\nAt $h=3$: $8-6=\\pi(9)\\frac{dh}{dt}$, giving\n$$\\frac{dh}{dt}=\\frac{2}{9\\pi}\\approx0.071\\text{ ft/min}.$$\n\n**Answer** $\\frac{dh}{dt}=\\frac{2}{9\\pi}\\approx0.071$ ft/min (rising).\n\n**Sanity check** Positive since inflow $8>$ outflow $6$; near equilibrium at $h=4$ where $dh/dt\\to0$, consistent.\n\n**How an interviewer probes this** They might ask: at what depth does the level stop changing, and is that equilibrium stable?",
