    solution_walkthrough: "**Identify the problem type** Sliding-ladder related rates with a constraint linking a second point to the ladder.\n\n**Governing principle** Related rates via implicit differentiation of the Pythagorean constraint.\n\n**Assumptions** Ladder rigid length $13$; wall vertical, floor horizontal; painter's height is exactly half the top height $y$.\n\n**Execute** Constraint: $x^2+y^2=169$. At $x=5$, $y=\\sqrt{169-25}=12$. Differentiate:\n$$2x\\frac{dx}{dt}+2y\\frac{dy}{dt}=0\\Rightarrow \\frac{dy}{dt}=-\\frac{x}{y}\\frac{dx}{dt}=-\\frac{5}{12}(2)=-\\frac{5}{6}.$$\nPainter height $H=\\frac{y}{2}$, so\n$$\\frac{dH}{dt}=\\frac{1}{2}\\frac{dy}{dt}=-\\frac{5}{12}\\approx-0.417\\text{ ft/s}.$$\n\n**Answer** $\\frac{dH}{dt}=-\\frac{5}{12}\\approx-0.417$ ft/s (descending).\n\n**Sanity check** Top descends as base slides out; painter, at midpoint, descends at half that rate. Sign negative, magnitude smaller than $dy/dt$: consistent.\n\n**How an interviewer probes this** Expect: as $x\\to13$, why does $dy/dt\\to-\\infty$, and is that physical?",
