    tier: "intermediate",
    solution_walkthrough: "**Identify the problem type** A product of an algebraic factor $x$ and a trig factor $\\sin x$ that does not simplify under substitution: use integration by parts.\n\n**Governing principle** Integration by parts: $\\int u\\,dv = uv-\\int v\\,du$, choosing $u$ to be the factor that simplifies when differentiated (LIATE picks the algebraic $x$).\n\n**Assumptions** Integrand continuous on $[0,\\pi]$; FTC applies.\n\n**Execute** Let $u=x\\Rightarrow du=dx$ and $dv=\\sin x\\,dx\\Rightarrow v=-\\cos x$. Then $$\\int x\\sin x\\,dx=-x\\cos x+\\int\\cos x\\,dx=-x\\cos x+\\sin x.$$ Evaluate: at $\\pi$, $-\\pi\\cos\\pi+\\sin\\pi=\\pi(1)+0=\\pi$; at $0$, $0+0=0$.\n\n**Answer** $$\\int_{0}^{\\pi} x\\sin x\\,dx=\\pi\\approx3.14.$$\n\n**Sanity check** On $[0,\\pi]$ both $x$ and $\\sin x$ are nonnegative, so the result must be positive: it is.\n\n**How an interviewer probes this** Expect a follow-up asking you to justify the choice of $u$ (why not $u=\\sin x$?) or to extend to $\\int x^{2}\\sin x\\,dx$ requiring parts twice.",
