    created_at: "2026-07-01T00:00:00.000Z",
    time_limit_seconds: null,
    parts_json: null,
    tier: "intermediate",
    solution_walkthrough: "**Identify the problem type** Constrained minimal-surface-area optimization.\n\n**Governing principle** Minimize surface area subject to a fixed-volume constraint; set the derivative to zero.\n\n**Assumptions** Wall thickness negligible; a true closed cylinder (two circular caps).\n\n**Execute** Constraint: $V=\\pi r^2 h\\Rightarrow h=\\dfrac{V}{\\pi r^2}$. Surface area:\n$$S=2\\pi r^2+2\\pi r h=2\\pi r^2+\\frac{2V}{r}.$$\nDifferentiate: $S'(r)=4\\pi r-\\dfrac{2V}{r^2}=0\\Rightarrow r^3=\\dfrac{V}{2\\pi}$. Substituting back gives $h=\\dfrac{V}{\\pi r^2}=2r$.\n\n**Answer** The optimum is $h=2r$ (height equals diameter); with $V=500$, $r\\approx 4.30\\text{ cm}$, $h\\approx 8.60\\text{ cm}$.\n\n**Sanity check** $S''=4\\pi+4V/r^3>0$, confirming a minimum.\n\n**How an interviewer probes this** Likely: \"What if the can is open-topped (one cap)? How does $h/r$ change?\"",
