    first_principle_target: "Setting the first derivative to zero to locate an extremum",
    solution_outline: "$T(x)=x/8+\\sqrt{(60-x)^2+40^2}/2$. Set $T'=0$: $1/8=(60-x)/(2\\sqrt{(60-x)^2+1600})$. Let $u=60-x$: $2\\sqrt{u^2+1600}=8u\\Rightarrow u^2+1600=16u^2\\Rightarrow u^2=1600/15$, $u\\approx10.33$, so $x\\approx49.7\\text{ m}$.",
    major_category: "all",
    requires_diagram: true,
    created_at: "2026-07-01T00:00:00.000Z",
    time_limit_seconds: null,
    parts_json: null,
    tier: "intermediate",
    solution_walkthrough: "**Identify the problem type** Fastest-path (least-time) optimization across two media.\n\n**Governing principle** Minimize total time by setting the first derivative to zero (Snell's-law form).\n\n**Assumptions** Constant speeds in each medium; straight-line swim segment; still water.\n\n**Execute** Running distance $=x$, swim distance $=\\sqrt{(60-x)^2+40^2}$:\n$$T(x)=\\frac{x}{8}+\\frac{\\sqrt{(60-x)^2+1600}}{2}.$$\nWith $u=60-x$, $T'=0$ gives\n$$\\frac{1}{8}=\\frac{u}{2\\sqrt{u^2+1600}}\\Rightarrow 2\\sqrt{u^2+1600}=8u.$$\nSquaring: $u^2+1600=16u^2\\Rightarrow u^2=\\tfrac{1600}{15}\\Rightarrow u\\approx10.3\\text{ m}$.\n\n**Answer** $x=60-u\\approx 49.7\\text{ m}$ down the shore.\n\n**Sanity check** Since running is much faster, she should run most of the way and enter near the point closest to $B$ ($x=60$); $x\\approx 49.7$ is sensibly close to that.\n\n**How an interviewer probes this** Expect: \"Show your condition is exactly Snell's law, $\\sin\\theta_1/v_1=\\sin\\theta_2/v_2$.\"",
    assumption_challenges: ["You assumed the swim segment is a straight line through still water. With a shoreline current pushing the swimmer sideways, would the least-time entry point move up or down the beach?"],
