    subtopic: "Derivatives",
    difficulty: 3,
    prompt_text: "The curve $x^{3}+y^{3}=6xy$ (the folium of Descartes) passes through the point $(3,3)$. Find the slope $\\dfrac{dy}{dx}$ at that point, and state whether the tangent line there is horizontal, vertical, or neither.",
    first_principle_target: "Implicit differentiation",
    solution_outline: "Implicit differentiate: $3x^2+3y^2 y'=6y+6x y'$, so $y'=\\frac{6y-3x^2}{3y^2-6x}=\\frac{2y-x^2}{y^2-2x}$. At $(3,3)$: $y'=(6-9)/(9-6)=-1$. Tangent is neither horizontal nor vertical.",
