    requires_diagram: false,
    created_at: "2026-07-01T00:00:00.000Z",
    time_limit_seconds: null,
    parts_json: null,
    tier: "intermediate",
    solution_walkthrough: "**Identify the problem type** $y$ is defined implicitly; use implicit differentiation.\n\n**Governing principle** Differentiate both sides with respect to $x$, treating $y=y(x)$ and applying the chain rule to every $y$ term.\n\n**Assumptions** Near $(3,3)$ the curve locally defines a differentiable $y(x)$ (denominator nonzero).\n\n**Execute** Differentiate:\n$$3x^{2}+3y^{2}\\frac{dy}{dx}=6y+6x\\frac{dy}{dx}.$$\nCollect $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ terms:\n$$\\frac{dy}{dx}\\left(3y^{2}-6x\\right)=6y-3x^{2}\\;\\Rightarrow\\;\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{2y-x^{2}}{y^{2}-2x}.$$\nAt $(3,3)$: $\\dfrac{2(3)-9}{9-6}=\\dfrac{-3}{3}=-1$.\n\n**Answer** $\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=-1$; the tangent has slope $-1$, so it is neither horizontal nor vertical.\n\n**Sanity check** Denominator $y^2-2x=3\\neq0$, so slope is finite and well-defined; symmetry of the curve about $y=x$ makes slope $-1$ at the symmetric point believable.\n\n**How an interviewer probes this** \"Where is the tangent horizontal?\" (set numerator $2y-x^2=0$) or \"vertical?\" (denominator $=0$).",
